== THE SIXTH STAGE

Now when they had got almost all the way out of this wilderness, Faithful happened to glance back, and saw someone coming after them, and he knew him. Oh! Faithful said to his brother, who is that coming over there? Then Christian looked, and said, It is my good friend Evangelist. Yes, and my good friend too, said Faithful, for it was he who set me on the way to the gate. By now Evangelist had come up to them, and he greeted them like this.

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: Peace be with you#super[+], dearly beloved, and peace be to your#super[+] helpers.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Welcome, welcome, my good Evangelist! The sight of your face brings back to me your kindness of long ago and your tireless labors for my eternal good.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: And a thousand times welcome, said good Faithful. Your company, O sweet Evangelist, how dear it is to us poor pilgrims!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: Then Evangelist said, How have things gone with you#super[+], my friends, since we last parted? What have you#super[+] met with, and how have you#super[+] behaved yourselves#super[+]?]

Then Christian and Faithful told him everything that had happened to them on the way, and how, and with what difficulty, they had reached that place.

I am very glad, said Evangelist, not that you#super[+] have met with trials, but that you#super[+] have been victors, and that, in spite of many weaknesses, you#super[+] have kept on in the way to this very day.

I say, I am very glad of this, and that for my own sake and for yours#super[+]: I have sown, and you#super[+] have reaped; and the day is coming when “both he who sows and they who reap will rejoice together,” John 4:36; that is, if you#super[+] hold out: “for in due time you#super[+] will reap a harvest if you#super[+] do not give up.” Gal 6:9. The crown is before you#super[+], and it is an incorruptible one; “run in such a way as to obtain it.” 1 Cor 9:24–27. There are some who set out for this crown, and after they have gone a long way for it, another comes in and takes it from them: “hold fast, therefore, to what you#super[+] have, so that no one will take your#super[+] crown.” Rev 3:11. You#super[+] are not yet out of the devil’s gunshot; “you#super[+] have not yet resisted to the point of shedding your#super[+] blood, in your#super[+] struggle against sin.” Let the kingdom be always before you#super[+], and believe steadfastly in the things that are invisible. Let nothing on this side of the other world get inside you#super[+]. And above all, look well to your#super[+] own hearts and to their lusts; for they are “deceitful above all things and desperately wicked.” Set your#super[+] faces like a flint; you#super[+] have all power in heaven and earth on your#super[+] side.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian thanked him for his exhortations, but told him also that they wanted him to speak further to them, to help them for the rest of the way; and all the more because they knew well that he was a prophet, and could tell them of things that might happen to them, and also how they might resist and overcome them. Faithful also agreed to this request. So Evangelist began as follows.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: My sons, you#super[+] have heard in the word of the truth of the Gospel that you#super[+] must “endure many hardships to enter the kingdom of God”; and again, that “in town after town chains and afflictions await you#super[+]”; and so you#super[+] cannot expect to go long on your#super[+] pilgrimage without them, of one kind or another. You#super[+] have already found something of the truth of these testimonies for yourselves#super[+], and more will follow immediately. For now, as you#super[+] see, you#super[+] are almost out of this wilderness, and so you#super[+] will soon come into a town that you#super[+] will shortly see ahead of you#super[+]\; and in that town you#super[+] will be hard pressed by enemies, who will strain hard to kill you#super[+]. And you#super[+] can be sure that one or both of you#super[+] must seal the testimony you#super[+] hold with blood; but “be faithful even unto death, and the King will give you#super[+] a crown of life.” He who dies there, although his death will be violent and his pain perhaps great, will still have the better of his companion; not only because he will have arrived at the Celestial City sooner, but because he will escape many miseries that the other will meet with in the rest of his journey. But when you#super[+] have come to the town, and find fulfilled what I have told you#super[+] here, then remember your#super[+] friend, and acquit yourselves#super[+] like men, and “entrust your#super[+] souls to your#super[+] faithful Creator and continue to do good.”]

Then I saw in my dream that when they had got out of the wilderness, they at once saw a town before them, and the name of that town is Vanity; and at the town a fair is held, called Vanity Fair. It is held all year long. It bears the name of Vanity Fair because the town where it is held is lighter than vanity, Ps 62:9; and also because all that is sold there, or that comes there, is vanity; in the words of the wise, “All that comes is vanity.” Eccl 11:8; see also 1:2-14; 2:11-17; Isa 40:17.

This fair is no newly founded affair, but a thing of ancient standing. I will show you how it began.

Almost five thousand years ago there were pilgrims walking to the Celestial City, as these two honest men are; and Beelzebub, Apollyon, and Legion, with their companions, seeing by the path the pilgrims made that their way to the city lay through this town of Vanity, schemed to set up a fair here: a fair in which all sorts of vanity would be sold, and which would last all year long. So at this fair all such merchandise is sold as houses, lands, trades, positions, honors, preferments, titles, countries, kingdoms, lusts, pleasures; and delights of all sorts, such as harlots, wives, husbands, children, masters, servants, lives, blood, bodies, souls, silver, gold, pearls, precious stones, and what not.

And besides, at this fair there are at all times conjuring tricks, cheats, games, plays, fools, apes, rascals, and rogues to be seen, and of every kind.

Here too, and for nothing, can be seen thefts, murders, adulteries, perjurers, and all of a blood-red color.

And as in other fairs of less importance there are separate rows and streets under their own names, where such and such wares are sold, so here likewise you have particular places, rows, and streets (namely, countries and kingdoms), where the wares of this fair are most readily found. Here is the Britain Row, the French Row, the Italian Row, the Spanish Row, the German Row, where various sorts of vanities are to be sold. But as in other fairs one particular commodity is the chief of the whole fair, so the ware of Rome and her merchandise is greatly promoted in this fair; only our English nation, with some others, has taken a dislike to it.

Now, as I said, the way to the Celestial City lies right through this town, where this lively fair is held; and he who wants to go to the city, and yet not go through this town, “would have to leave this world.” 1 Cor 5:10. The Prince of princes himself, when he was here, went through this town to his own country, and on a fair-day too; yes, and I think it was Beelzebub, the chief lord of this fair, who invited him to buy some of his vanities; indeed, he would have made him lord of the fair, if only he would have paid him homage as he went through the town. Indeed, because he was such a person of honor, Beelzebub took him from street to street, and showed him all the kingdoms of the world in a short time, so that he might, if possible, lure that blessed One into bargaining for and buying some of his vanities; but he had no interest in the merchandise, and so he left the town without spending so much as one farthing on these vanities. Matt 4:8, 9; Luke 4:5–7. This fair, therefore, is an ancient thing, of long standing, and a very great fair.

Now these pilgrims, as I said, had to go through this fair. Well, so they did; but look, even as they entered the fair, all the people in the fair were stirred, and the town itself was, as it were, in a hubbub about them; and that for several reasons: for,

First, The Pilgrims were dressed in a kind of clothing different from the clothing of anyone who traded in that fair. So the people of the fair stared hard at them: some said they were fools, 1 Cor 4:9, 10; some, that they were madmen; and some, that they were foreigners.

Secondly, And as they wondered at their clothes, so they wondered at their speech too; for few could understand what they said. They naturally spoke the language of Canaan, but those who ran the fair were the men of this world; so that from one end of the fair to the other, they seemed like barbarians to each other. 1 Cor 2:7, 8.

Thirdly, But what puzzled the merchants not a little was that these pilgrims thought very little of all their wares. They did not even care to look at them; and if the merchants called on them to buy, they would put their fingers in their ears, and cry, “Turn away my eyes from beholding vanity,” Ps 119:37, and look upward, signifying that their trade and commerce was in heaven. Phil 3:20, 21.

One man, seeing how the men carried themselves, happened to say to them mockingly, “What will you#super[+] buy?” But they looked at him gravely and said, “We buy the truth.” Prov 23:23. That gave the people an occasion to despise the men all the more; some mocked, some taunted, some spoke insultingly, and some called on others to strike them. At last things came to an uproar and a great stir in the fair, so that all order broke down. Now word was brought at once to the great one of the fair, who quickly came down, and appointed some of his most trusted friends to question the men over whom the fair had almost been turned upside down. So the men were brought in to be questioned; and those who sat in judgment on them asked them where they came from, where they were going, and what they were doing there in such unusual dress. The men told them that they were pilgrims and strangers in the world, and that they were going to their own country, which was the heavenly Jerusalem, Heb 11:13–16; and that they had given the men of the town no cause, nor the merchants either, to abuse them like this and to hinder them on their journey, unless it was that, when one asked them what they would buy, they said they would buy the truth. But those who were appointed to question them believed they were nothing other than lunatics and madmen, or else people who had come to throw everything in the fair into confusion. So they took them and beat them, and smeared them with dirt, and then put them into the cage, so that they might be made a spectacle to all the men of the fair. There they lay for some time, and were made the objects of any man’s sport, or malice, or revenge, while the great one of the fair laughed all the while at everything that happened to them. But the men were patient, “not repaying insult with insult, but with blessing,” and giving good words for bad, and kindness for injuries done; and some men in the fair, who were more observant and less prejudiced than the rest, began to rebuke and blame the baser sort for the continual abuse they were heaping on the men. The baser sort, in turn, lashed out at them angrily, counting them as bad as the men in the cage, and telling them that they seemed to be in league with them, and should be made to share their misfortunes. The others replied that, for all they could see, the men were quiet and sober, and meant nobody any harm; and that there were many who traded in their fair who deserved to be put into the cage, yes, and the pillory too, more than the men they had abused. So, after many words had passed on both sides (the men behaving very wisely and soberly before them all the while), they came to blows among themselves, and did one another harm. Then these two poor men were brought before their examiners again, and were charged with being guilty of the recent hubbub in the fair. So they beat them pitifully, and loaded them with irons, and led them in chains up and down the fair, as an example and a terror to others, so that no one would speak on their behalf or join them. But Christian and Faithful behaved yet more wisely, and took the disgrace and shame heaped on them with so much meekness and patience that it won several of the men in the fair to their side (though only a few compared with the rest). This put the other party into a still greater rage, so that they resolved on the death of these two men. So they threatened that neither cage nor irons would be enough for them, but that the two should die for the harm they had done, and for deceiving the men of the fair.

Then they were sent back to the cage again, until further orders should be given about them. So they put them in, and fastened their feet in the stocks.

Here also they called to mind again what they had heard from their faithful friend Evangelist, and were all the more strengthened in their way and their sufferings by what he had told them would happen to them. They also now comforted each other with this: that whichever of them it fell to suffer, he would have the best of it; so each man secretly wished that he might have that honor. But committing themselves to the all-wise ordering of Him who rules all things, they remained very contentedly in the condition they were in, until something else should be done with them.

Then, when a suitable time had been set, they brought them out for their trial, with a view to condemning them. When the time had come, they were brought before their enemies and formally charged. The judge’s name was Lord Hate-good. Their indictment was one and the same in substance, though it varied somewhat in form; and its contents were these: “That they were enemies to, and disturbers of, the trade; that they had made commotions and divisions in the town, and had won a party to their own most dangerous opinions, in contempt of the law of their prince.”

Then Faithful began to answer that he had only set himself against what had set itself against Him who is higher than the highest. And, he said, as for disturbance, I make none, since I am a man of peace myself; the people who were won to us were won by seeing our truth and innocence, and they have only been turned from the worse to the better. And as for the king you talk of, since he is Beelzebub, the enemy of our Lord, I defy him and all his angels.

Then a proclamation was made that those who had anything to say for their lord the king against the prisoner at the bar should appear at once and give their evidence. So three witnesses came in, namely, Envy, Superstition, and Pickthank. They were then asked if they knew the prisoner at the bar, and what they had to say against him for their lord the king.

Then Envy stepped forward and spoke to this effect: My lord, I have known this man a long time, and will testify on oath before this honorable bench that he is—

#speech[#smallcaps[Judge]: Wait; swear him in.]

So they swore him in. Then he said, My lord, this man, in spite of his fair-sounding name, is one of the vilest men in our country. He respects neither prince nor people, law nor custom, but does all he can to fill all men’s heads with certain disloyal notions of his, which in general he calls principles of faith and holiness. And in particular, I once heard him myself declare that Christianity and the customs of our town of Vanity were diametrically opposed, and could not be reconciled. By that saying, my lord, he condemns at one stroke not only all our praiseworthy doings, but us for doing them.

Then the judge said to him, Do you have anything more to say?

#speech[#smallcaps[Envy]: My lord, I could say much more, only I do not wish to be tedious to the court. Yet if need be, when the other gentlemen have given their evidence, rather than let anything be lacking that will finish him off, I will add to my testimony against him. So he was told to stand aside.]

Then they called Superstition, and told him to look at the prisoner. They also asked what he could say against him for their lord the king. Then they swore him in, and he began.

#speech[#smallcaps[Superstition]: My lord, I am not well acquainted with this man, nor do I want to know him any better. However, this I know, that he is a very pestilent fellow, from some talk I had with him the other day in this town; for then, talking with him, I heard him say that our religion was worthless, and such that a man could by no means please God by it. Your lordship knows very well, my lord, what must necessarily follow from that saying of his, namely, that we always worship in vain, are still in our sins, and in the end will be damned; and that is what I have to say.]

Then Pickthank was sworn in, and told to say what he knew against the prisoner at the bar on behalf of their lord the king.

#speech[#smallcaps[Pickthank]: My lord, and all you gentlemen, I have known this fellow a long time, and have heard him say things that ought not to be said; for he has railed against our noble prince Beelzebub, and has spoken with contempt of his honorable friends, whose names are the Lord Old Man, the Lord Carnal Delight, the Lord Luxurious, the Lord Desire of Vain Glory, my old Lord Lechery, Sir Having Greedy, with all the rest of our nobility. And he has said, moreover, that if all men were of his mind, then, if possible, not one of these noblemen would be allowed to exist in this town any longer. Besides that, he has not been afraid to rail against you, my lord, who are now appointed to be his judge, calling you an ungodly villain, with many other such slanderous names, with which he has spattered most of the gentry of our town.]

When this Pickthank had told his tale, the judge turned to the prisoner at the bar and said, You renegade, heretic, and traitor, have you heard what these honest gentlemen have testified against you?

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: May I say a few words in my own defense?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Judge]: Sirrah, sirrah, you deserve to live no longer, but to be put to death on the spot; yet, so that all men may see how gentle we are toward you, let us hear what you, vile renegade, have to say.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: 1\. I say, then, in answer to what Mr. Envy has said, I never said anything but this: that whatever rules, or laws, or customs, or people are flatly against the word of God are diametrically opposed to Christianity. If I have said anything wrong in this, show me my error, and I am ready here before you to take it back.]

2\. As to the second, namely Mr. Superstition and his charge against me, I said only this: that in the worship of God a divine faith is required; but there can be no divine faith without a divine revelation of the will of God. Therefore whatever is thrust into the worship of God that does not agree with divine revelation can only be done by a human faith; and that faith will be of no benefit for eternal life.

3\. As to what Mr. Pickthank has said, I say (leaving aside such charges as that I am said to rail, and the like) that the prince of this town, with all the rabble, his attendants, named by this gentleman, are more fit to live in hell than in this town and country. And so may the Lord have mercy on me.

Then the judge called to the jury (who had stood by all this while to hear and observe), Gentlemen of the jury, you#super[+] see this man about whom so great an uproar has been made in this town; you#super[+] have also heard what these worthy gentlemen have testified against him; and you#super[+] have heard his reply and confession. It now rests with you#super[+] to hang him or to save his life; but I think it right to instruct you#super[+] in our law.

A law was passed in the days of Pharaoh the Great, servant to our prince, that, for fear those of a contrary religion should multiply and grow too strong for him, their males should be thrown into the river. Exod 1:22. A law was also passed in the days of Nebuchadnezzar the Great, another of his servants, that whoever would not fall down and worship his golden image should be thrown into a fiery furnace. Dan 3:6. A law was also passed in the days of Darius, that whoever for a certain time called upon any god but him should be cast into the lions’ den. Dan 6:7. Now this rebel has broken the substance of these laws, not only in thought (which is not to be endured), but also in word and deed; and that must therefore be utterly intolerable.

As for Pharaoh’s law, it was made on a supposition, to prevent harm, when no crime was yet apparent; but here a crime is apparent. As for the second and third, you#super[+] see he argues against our religion; and for the treason he has already confessed, he deserves to be put to death.

Then the jury went out. Their names were Mr. Blindman, Mr. No-good, Mr. Malice, Mr. Love-lust, Mr. Live-loose, Mr. Heady, Mr. High-mind, Mr. Enmity, Mr. Liar, Mr. Cruelty, Mr. Hate-light, and Mr. Implacable; each one of them gave his own verdict against him in private among themselves, and afterward they unanimously agreed to find him guilty before the judge. And first among themselves, Mr. Blindman, the foreman, said, I see clearly that this man is a heretic. Then Mr. No-good said, Away with such a fellow from the earth. Yes, said Mr. Malice, for I hate the very look of him. Then Mr. Love-lust said, I could never stand him. Nor I, said Mr. Live-loose, for he was forever condemning my way of life. Hang him, hang him, said Mr. Heady. A miserable wretch, said Mr. High-mind. My heart rises against him, said Mr. Enmity. He is a rogue, said Mr. Liar. Hanging is too good for him, said Mr. Cruelty. Let us get him out of the way, said Mr. Hate-light. Then Mr. Implacable said, If I were given the whole world, I could not be reconciled to him; so let us at once bring in a verdict of guilty, and of death.

And so they did; so he was at once condemned to be taken from the place where he was to the place he had come from, and there to be put to the most cruel death that could be devised.

So they brought him out, to deal with him according to their law; and first they scourged him, then they struck him with their fists, then they slashed his flesh with knives; after that they stoned him with stones, then jabbed him with their swords; and last of all they burned him to ashes at the stake. So Faithful came to his end.

Now I saw that there stood behind the crowd a chariot and a pair of horses waiting for Faithful, who (as soon as his adversaries had put him to death) was taken up into it, and at once was carried up through the clouds with the sound of a trumpet, the nearest way to the celestial gate. But as for Christian, he had some respite, and was sent back to prison; so he stayed there for a while. But he who overrules all things, having the power of their rage in his own hand, so brought it about that Christian escaped them for that time, and went his way.

And as he went, he sang these words,

#quote[“Well, Faithful, thou hast faithfully profest \
Unto thy Lord, with whom thou shalt be blest, \
When faithless ones, with all their vain delights, \
Are crying out under their hellish plights: \
Sing, Faithful, sing, and let thy name survive; \
For though they killed thee, thou art yet alive.”]
